      "text": "### Lignocellulose Degradation"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#010",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nPLANT CELL WALL COMPOSITION:\n  Cellulose: 40–50% of dry weight; crystalline glucose polymer\n  Hemicellulose: 25–35%; branched polysaccharides (xylan, mannan)\n  Lignin: 20–30%; complex aromatic polymer; extremely resistant\n    → Lignin: three-dimensional network of phenylpropanoid units\n    → NO animal can digest lignin (no animals produce ligninases)\n    → Only white rot fungi and some bacteria can break it down"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#011",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "WHITE ROT FUNGI (Basidiomycetes primarily):\n  Degrade BOTH cellulose AND lignin\n  Leave white/pale fibrous residue (cellulose after lignin removed)\n  Enzymes: lignin peroxidase, manganese peroxidase, laccase\n    → All oxidative, peroxide-dependent enzymes\n    → Radicals attack the phenolic bonds of lignin\n  Examples: Trametes versicolor (turkey tail), Pleurotus (oyster mushroom),\n    Phanerochaete chrysosporium (research model)"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#012",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "BROWN ROT FUNGI (Basidiomycetes):\n  Degrade cellulose + hemicellulose; leave LIGNIN intact\n  Residue: brown, cubic-cracking (lignin skeleton remains)\n  Mechanism: H₂O₂-driven Fenton chemistry (non-enzymatic cellulose attack)\n  Examples: Poria, Postia, Serpula lacrymans (dry rot)"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#013",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "DRY ROT (Serpula lacrymans):\n  Can transport water through hyphae for many meters\n  → Attack dry timber that couldn't otherwise sustain fungal growth\n  → Major building deterioration problem; extremely hard to eradicate\n  → Mycelial strands penetrate through masonry\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#014",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
