      "text": "### Carbon Cycle Role"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#015",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nFUNGAL CARBON CYCLE CONTRIBUTION:\n  1. Fungi mine recalcitrant carbon (lignin, cellulose)\n     → Returns C to atmosphere as CO₂\n  2. Mycorrhizal fungi: plants allocate 10–30% of photosynthate to mycorrhizae\n     → Substantial C transfer from atmosphere to soil\n  3. Fungal necromass: dead fungal biomass is major input to soil organic matter\n     → Chitin decomposes slowly → soil carbon storage\n  4. Glomalin: glycoprotein from AM fungi; ~27% of soil organic carbon\n     → Soil aggregation; important for soil structure"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#016",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "WITHOUT FUNGI:\n  Permafrost carbon: ancient plant carbon locked in permafrost\n  As permafrost thaws → brown rot fungi could decompose it\n  → Positive feedback loop: warming → decomposition → more CO₂ → more warming\n  This is a significant climate tipping point concern\n```"
    },
