      },
      "text": "### Arbuscular Mycorrhizae (AM)"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#025",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nARBUSCULAR MYCORRHIZAL (AM) FUNGI (Glomeromycota):\n  Most common mycorrhizal type: ~80% of land plant species\n  Hyphae penetrate INTO root cortex cells → form arbuscules\n  Arbuscule: tree-like branching structure inside cell\n  → Huge surface area for carbon/phosphorus exchange at cell-fungal interface\n  Periarbuscular membrane: plant plasma membrane invaginates around arbuscule\n  → Not inside cytoplasm; surrounded by membrane \"apoplast\""
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#026",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "OBLIGATE: cannot live without a plant host; cannot be cultured alone\n  ANCIENT: fossil evidence in Devonian roots (410 Ma)\n    → Among the oldest terrestrial symbioses known\n    → May have enabled land colonization by early plants"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#027",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "PARTNERS: wide range of plants (grasses, crops, most non-woody plants)\n    Wheat, corn, rice, tomato, clover: all AM-dependent\n  FUNGI: ~250 species (Glomus/Rhizophagus, Funneliformis, Diversispora, etc.)"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#028",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "AGRICULTURAL IMPORTANCE:\n  AM fungi are the primary route of phosphorus uptake for most crops\n  Conventional agriculture: heavy phosphate fertilization → suppresses AM\n  → Crops become dependent on fertilizer, not mycorrhizae\n  Organic agriculture: preserve AM communities → better P use efficiency\n  Inoculant products: Glomus inoculants sold for revegetation, agriculture\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#029",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
