      },
      "text": "### The Wood Wide Web"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#030",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Suzanne Simard's work (1997, Nature) demonstrated that carbon moves between trees through mycorrhizal networks — \"Mother Trees\" send carbon to seedlings."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#031",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nTHE MYCORRHIZAL NETWORK:\n  Individual tree roots → connected to multiple fungal partners\n  Same fungal individual → connected to multiple tree roots\n  → Network of carbon/nutrient exchange at ecosystem scale"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#032",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "SIMARD'S FINDINGS (1997):\n  Radiolabeled CO₂ fed to birch → ¹⁴C appeared in Douglas fir\n  → Carbon moved from birch to fir via mycorrhizal network\n  Mechanism: net carbon flow from source (high photosynthate, light)\n    to sink (shaded, low photosynthate)\n  → \"Altruism\" in the popular press; biology of resource allocation"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-ECOLOGY.md#033",
      "path": [
        "03-ECOLOGY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:mycology:03-ecology, git-history:mycology:03-ecology]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "concepts": "[ecology]",
        "current_path": "mycology/03-ECOLOGY.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:ecology",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ecology]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Fungal Ecology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE REAL BIOLOGY:\n  NOT \"trees talking\" or \"forest internet\"\n  It IS: fungal hyphae connecting root systems, allowing bidirectional\n    flow of carbon and nutrients between trees\n  Carbon moves DOWN gradients (source-sink):\n    → Sun-lit canopy trees → shaded seedlings (Simard's main finding)\n    → Dying trees → release carbon to network? (less well established)\n  Phosphorus and water also move through networks\n  Defense signals? Tentative evidence; far less certain than C/P exchange"
    }
  ],
